From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dan Carney <dcarney@cloudflare.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: Leave the SharedFunctionInfo id out of the read-only heap checksum

A read-only snapshot holds the next SharedFunctionInfo unique id of the
isolate that took it. An embedder custom startup snapshot of the
shared read-only heap therefore differs from the built-in one only in
that id, yet:

* DEBUG builds CHECK that every isolate of a group sets the read-only
  heap up from a snapshot with the same checksum, which fails as soon as
  isolates restored from the two kinds of snapshot share a group.
* An isolate that joins an existing read-only heap takes its next id
  from the snapshot that heap came from, so an isolate restored from a
  snapshot whose isolate numbered more SharedFunctionInfos hands those
  ids out a second time.

Write the id first, as a fixed-width uint32 (it used to come last,
before the padding, as a variable-length integer), leave it out of the
checksum, and have an isolate joining an existing read-only heap
continue past its own snapshot's id.

diff --git a/src/execution/isolate.h b/src/execution/isolate.h
--- a/src/execution/isolate.h
+++ b/src/execution/isolate.h
@@ -1880,6 +1880,17 @@
     CHECK(successfully_exchanged);
   }
 
+  // Raises the next unique id to `id` if it is lower. For an isolate whose
+  // startup snapshot was taken by an isolate that numbered more
+  // SharedFunctionInfos than the one the shared read-only heap came from.
+  void EnsureNextUniqueSfiIdAtLeast(uint32_t id) {
+    uint32_t current = next_unique_sfi_id_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
+    while (current < id && !next_unique_sfi_id_.compare_exchange_weak(
+                               current, id, std::memory_order_relaxed,
+                               std::memory_order_relaxed)) {
+    }
+  }
+
